Z||000004||Neverfinished||04-16-2001||09:10 PM||||Also, <BR>Before I had my car painted pearl mettalic purple...<BR>My dad decided to move it into the garage.<BR>He put the car in neutral and took off the e-brake expecting it to coast into the garage...<BR>well he forgot about the steering lock and my wheels cranked a little bit the the left.<BR>equals......................................<BR>one new front fascia.<BR>why don't we start this thread on the worst car related screw ups you've ever had.<BR>I'm sure not everyone on this forum has a perfect track record!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||fyerstarter||04-16-2001||11:28 PM||||When I was 16 me and my friend were in my 69 mustang. He had just got in as I was backing up and hadn't shut the door all the way yet. Little did we know my dad's pickup truck was parked right there :P Bent the door all they way back (or forward rather) till it was parralell with the front fender.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mattsfiero||04-17-2001||08:16 AM||||How to end a hard day.<P>It is getting colder outside and I am leaving soon I think I will reach through the open window and warm up the car.<P>Place keys in the ignition<BR>Check car and be sure it is in Neutral<BR>Turn key slightly to be sure it is neutral<BR>Cut the Ignition because you can feel the car jump slightly.<BR>Only problem is the Starter celiuniod is now gone and it is still turning the starter and the car starts to move forward even after the keys are removed.<BR>Pull the E Brake only to find out that the car is pulling to hard as it slams into a barn door and a 80's Caprice Classic.<P>Finally manage to get in the car and press down the clutch only to hear the engine crank as you pull it in to neutral to run around open the deck lid and remove the negative terminal Wire to shut it down. Back the car up and asses damage.<P>------------------<BR>Matt<BR>85 custom coupe<BR>Mattsfiero@aol.com<BR>www.anglefire.com/ma3/mattsfiero||||reg||9
Z||000007||TheFiero64||04-17-2001||10:05 AM||||LMAO Mattsfiero, I watched you do that one. Good thing Fieros are pliable.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg"> <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||10
Z||000008||Brygida Fieros||04-17-2001||10:35 AM||||I only had this car for a month and a half or so, when I screw it up when I got in a pretty bad accident. One night, dark night, after midnight, me in my Fiero GT and my bf (at the time, now my exbf) in his Porsche 911 Targa<BR>top. We were coming back from friend's house after a jaccuzzi bath. We were street racing alittle. The road was curving. The sprinkles came on to water the grass and made the road wet and slippery. As my bf passed me, my car shook alittle, fish-tailed for few seconds then I partially lost control of the back. I spinned backwards and hit the curb of the<BR>road, then I lost total control and don't remember what happened next.<BR>Only from my bf's tells I know kinda what happened. He saw everything in his rear-view mirror and was horrified what he saw. He couldn't believe his eyes and was stoned. He also was scared that he might find me dead in the car. He told me, he saw my car hitting the curb really hard, all 4-wheels off the road. Spinning backwards, my car (driver's<BR>side) hit hard (50-60 miles/hr one of the royal FL palm trees on the side of the road. Cracked the palm tree in a half and bent it, but still standing solid. Glass and parts of the car flying everywhere on impact. My decklid w/ spoiler flew off the car. Then my car disappeared from his sight. He stopped his Porsche, took his shoes off, his legs<BR>shaking, he ran to the palm tree where I hit. He saw all the glass, parts and the cracked palm tree, but no sight of my car. There was<BR>alittle hill, he climbed up and looked down. There was a canal. Muddy water and darkness everywhere. He saw my car's headlights still shining out of the water. Car totally submerged in this black water. Without<BR>thinking he jumped in and searched for my sunroof. Luckily my sunroof was gone and the hole in the roof helped him find me quicker, still in the car. He felt me and pulled me up, dragged me up the hill, and sat me up on the ground, right next to the palm tree where I hit. The police and ambulance was there within 3-4 minutes. He tried CPR on me,<BR>but I was hurt and unconcious. He was afraid not to hurt me more. I was put on a stretcher & ambulance took me to the nearest hospital where my bf arrived shortly after. It took them over 2 hours to get my car out of the canal. 2 divers from the towing company. Stuff was everywhere in the water. Parts and my belonging.<BR>I sustained 3 broken ribs, broken right wrist in 3 places, lacerations to my chest from the seat belt that saved my life, concussion, cuts and bruises on my legs and back, but I & the palm tree survived. The car wasn't so lucky. It was severed in a half and totalled.<BR>I was in the hospital for about 6 hours, then released. I couldn't really breath with broken ribs and couldn't walk for about 2 weeks. The ribs healed up after about 2 months. They wanted to operate on my wrist<BR>but I told them to put it in a cast for few weeks.  3 days after this horrible accident, I managed to go and see my car. It was unbelievable. The car's frame was broken in a half in the back. The car was cricked<BR>with a huge dent in the post on the driver's side. Mudd and sea weed everywhere. 2 months later, I bought another red 88GT V6. Almost the same, but with<BR>less miles. A year after that I bought white 88GT V6 5-speed. And last<BR>year I bought the silver GT. Now I'm set, if I get in any more<BR>accidents and total one of my Fieros, I'd have 2 more.<BR>(That was a long story... but it was a big screw up)...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Brygida T.<BR>Palm Beach Fieros<BR>http://www.angelfire.com/fl3/palmbeachfieros||||reg||11
Z||000009||MontanaFiero||04-17-2001||03:50 PM||||Wow, Thank God he was able to get you out! <P>My dumbest car related mishap was when I was in high school working on my 81 chevy pickup. I had a remote starter button hooked up and the transmission was in reverse. I don't think the motor ever started on the first try before that time, must have done a good job tuning it up because it took off right out of the garage and accross the street into the neighbors (empty) driveway with me chasing after it.  I was able to get into it and apply the brake.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://members.home.net/blee291/fiero/mtfieroplate.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mattsfiero||04-17-2001||09:08 PM||||Wow that was a hell of a mishap Brygida I am glad you made it through that okay. I guess your lucky someone was there to help and you didn't give up on the car of cars.<P>Lets try to keep every one safe. It would not be good to loose someone that way.<P>------------------<BR>Matt<BR>85 custom coupe<BR>Mattsfiero@aol.com<BR>www.anglefire.com/ma3/mattsfiero||||reg||4
Z||000011||2xsess||04-18-2001||12:58 PM||||Wow Brygida, quite a story, those damn canals are scary. I used to live in Wellington,FL. had my 88GT down there too, hated those sprinkles, always messing up a nice wash and wax job. Even worse on a motorcycle, gets you wet in the process. <P>Well, here's my stupid story. <BR>Not my car, it was a company car. 5speed, Escort. Out on a delivery and decided to stop home for something. Stopped the car, put in in neutral,left it idling in the driveway. I was only running in to grab something. Ran in the house, grabbed whatever it was, ran out the front door and the car was gone, Started to freak out thinking someone had stolen the company car. Looked down the driveway and over to the neighbors to see if they were outside and if they had saw anything. Luckily they weren't outside or else they'd had seen the Escort in their yard about 6" from their house. I ran over to the car and hopped in, and drove real slowly off of their yard. I didn't go outside very much for a few weeks in fear of them asking me if I knew about the tire tracks across their yard.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>  <B>*For Sale*<BR>        $2000obo</B>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2xsess.gif">        <BR> <A HREF="http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html" TARGET=_blank>Visit My Website</A>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Raydar||04-18-2001||02:14 PM||||WOW, Brygida!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> I'm glad that you're still with us. Sounds like a close call. <BR>Pretty much the dumbest thing I've ever done involved a company vehicle (Chevy Astro van) that I drove, about 6 years ago. You have to understand that I work for the power company. Safety is (thankfully) drilled into our minds on an ongoing basis. <BR>On this particular day, we had just finished with a meeting. I climbed into my van. Started it up. Threw it in reverse. Whipped the wheel to the right. Stepped on the gas. And smacked a steel beam, encased in a foot of bright yellow-painted concrete, with the left front corner of my van. In front of half of my department, including the supervisors. I became the perfect "bad example".||||reg||11
Z||000013||mrfixit58||04-18-2001||05:24 PM||||At my last house, I built a 12'x30' work shop. The front 20 feet was a carport for my 69 Firebird convertible project. The back was a 12x12 workshop/storage area where I kept my tools, a small work bench, some household storage, and a chest freezer.<P>I had just finished replacing the floorboards in the convertible and decided to clean the floor. The car's battery was dead so I pushed it back and hooked the charger to it while I cleaned.<P>A couple of hours later, I decided to put the car back onto the carport and removed the charger and fired the motor. The carport had a steep apron so I needed to goose the throttle a little to get it up the incline. The car suddenly lurched forward so I slammed my foot on the brake pedal only to feel it hit the floorboard. Then... SMASH! right into the shop wall.<P>After my pulse slowed, I assessed the damage. Not too bad, a small crack in the headlight bezel on the car and a broken door jamb on the shop.<P>Well, unfortunately, that's not the end of the story. A couple of weeks later, my wife sent me out to the shop to get some steaks out of the freezer. When I opened the lid, the smell almost knocked me down! I seems that when I broke the shop wall, I also broke the electrical receptacle and the feed to the freezer. What a mess. And, imagine... my wife wouldn't even help me clean it out!<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||11
Z||000014||KRMFiero||04-18-2001||05:46 PM||||didnt want to say this but it kindof fits in here... <P>after i removed the engine cradle and still had it up about 4 foot in the air (resting on a piar of 2x4's (had cinderblocks under 2x4's to keep the back eng up, came back the next morning and saw two brokin 2x4's and a fiero lying on the floor of my garage.<P>oh yeah under the fiero was the upper half of my dads craftsman tool chest, competly crushed  (that cost me good money to replace)<P>Kyle<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (Red)<BR>1985 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE (White)<P>     <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/sig2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
